<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hendrik Carloff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hendrik Carloff was a merchant and colonial agent of the 17th century who served several companies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. For the Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Company he obtained land at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> from the king of Fetu (Efutu), where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Fort Carolusborg</a> was built in 1653. In 1658 he returned in Danish service and seized the Swedish forts, beginning the Danish Gold Coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hendrik-carloff.jpg" alt="Hendrik Carloff" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FortsGoldk%C3%BCste.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>By his own testimony Carloff was born in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and he began his career as a cabin boy. In 1637 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> employed him in Dutch Brazil, first as a soldier and then as a writer, and from 1645 to 1649 he served at Fort Elmina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nassau-gold-coast">Fort Nassau</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. Feigning illness, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> hoping to find backers for a plan of his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After twelve years with the company he entered the service of Louis de Geer, who founded the Swedish Africa Company at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>, as its commander and director. He occupied Butre in 1650, Anomabu in 1651 and Orsou in 1652. Sailing home in September 1652, his ship Christina, carrying about twenty bags of gold and 6,500 elephant teeth, was seized and taken to Plymouth. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> he was knighted on 3 May 1654.</p><p>On 27 March 1657 he went over to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> and became director-general of the Danish Africa Company. His ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gluckstadt">Glückstadt</a>, with 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and 48 crew, reached the coast on 25 January 1658 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Fort Carolusborg</a> two days later, when the gate was opened in the morning; the governor, Johann Philipp von Krusenstjerna, was arrested and about 185 kg of gold and several tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> were seized. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> this was a reason to break off peace talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p>From 1665 he advised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> and contracted to carry enslaved people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the French West Indies in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. In 1669 he sailed from Le Havre to Africa, where 1,000 slaves were embarked; over 750 were delivered in Martinique.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1676 he was appointed commissioner-general for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> plan to resettle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, abandoned after an English attack in 1672, and Jacob Binckes sailed with the expedition. Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ii-destrees">Jean d'Estrées</a> attacked the colony in 1677, and on 12 December a French cannonball set off the fortress's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> store, killing about 250 men. Carloff's own part is unclear: he had been at odds with Binckes and seems to have been taken into custody. Little more is recorded of him, and he died in or after 1677.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hendrik Carloff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Danish traders, led by <b>Hendrik Carloff</b>, arrived on the Gold Coast in 1658 and seized the Swedish forts.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-gold-coast">Danish Gold Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>Its agent <b>Hendrik Carloff</b> obtained land from the local Fante rulers, and in 1653 the company built Fort Carolusborg at Cape Coast, on the coast of modern Ghana, along with smaller lodges.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-gold-coast">Swedish Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rostock, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.08,12.13&amp;year=1664" aria-label="Rostock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4818 308 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hendrik Carloff"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.08,12.13&amp;year=1664">What was here in 1664? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
